[710] (w=71) [38;2;250;204;110m[1mThe honest take[22m[39m                                                        [0m]8;;
[711] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[712] (w=71) Your instinct (Arch ARM for native speed) is the wrong tool [3mfor Omarchy[0m]8;;
[713] (w=71) [3mspecifically[23m. Omarchy's heart and soul includes its curated package set[0m]8;;
[714] (w=71) and the omarchy mirror — all x86-only and deliberately so (guard       [0m]8;;
[715] (w=71) enforces it). Native ARM buys you perf but eviscerates the content you [0m]8;;
[716] (w=71) came for.                                                              [0m]8;;
[717] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[718] (w=71) Two coherent paths:                                                    [0m]8;;
[719] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[720] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m1. [39m[1mWant real Omarchy[22m → Rosetta amd64 route with official               [0m]8;;
[721] (w=71)    [38;2;218;186;250marchlinux/archlinux:base[39m. Slowest but complete. VNC via wayvnc as   [0m]8;;
[722] (w=71)    before.                                                             [0m]8;;
[723] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m2. [39m[1mWant fast native + "flavor"[22m → Arch ARM base, then you personally    [0m]8;;
[724] (w=71)    recreate the experience (dotfiles, config, apps, same scripts from  [0m]8;;
[725] (w=71)    [38;2;218;186;250mconfig/[39m which are arch-independent), skipping the omarchy mirror    [0m]8;;
[726] (w=71)    entirely. This is where your "own image of Omarchy" vision genuinely[0m]8;;
[727] (w=71)    diverges from upstream.                                             [0m]8;;
[728] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[729] (w=71) My recommendation, in case: [1mpivot the goal.[22m If you want                [0m]8;;
[730] (w=71) Omarchy-the-product, Rosetta. If the real value to you is the [3mQuattro  [0m]8;;
[731] (w=71) [3msetup/philosophy[23m (which is mostly arch-independent                     [0m]8;;
[732] (w=71) config/dotfiles/bin), native Arch ARM is the better base and you       [0m]8;;
[733] (w=71) reclaim Omarchy's soul by wiring the [38;2;218;186;250mconfig/[39m scripts directly, skipping[0m]8;;
[734] (w=71) dead hardware/bootloader modules.                                      [0m]8;;
[735] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[736] (w=71) Which way do you want vibes to lean — faithful product fidelity        [0m]8;;
[737] (w=71) (Rosetta) or fast native rebuild (Arch ARM)? That flips what I build   [0m]8;;
