[774] (w=71) [38;2;250;204;110m[1mWhat I actually know[22m[39m                                                   [0m]8;;
[775] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[776] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m- [39mRosetta 2 shipped with Big Sur (2020), Apple Silicon transition.     [0m]8;;
[777] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m- [39mLatest sign points the [3mother[23m direction: reports of "[1mRosetta 3[22m" in the[0m]8;;
[778] (w=71)   macOS 26 (Tahoe) seed lineage, with multi-threaded Intel translation [0m]8;;
[779] (w=71)   and big perf gains. So Apple appears to be lengthening Rosetta, not  [0m]8;;
[780] (w=71)   killing it.                                                          [0m]8;;
[781] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m- [39mApple has never issued a hard end-of-life / removal date.            [0m]8;;
[782] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m- [39mSo "sunsetting soon" is currently speculative. I'd bet on years, not [0m]8;;
[783] (w=71)   months.                                                              [0m]8;;
[784] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[785] (w=71) (Search backend's down mid-verification — that's my knowledge; worth a [0m]8;;
[786] (w=71) follow-up check before you commit real hours.)                         [0m]8;;
[787] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[788] (w=71) [38;2;250;204;110m[1mBut the strategic point survives anyway[22m[39m                                [0m]8;;
[789] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[790] (w=71) Even with no sunset: [1mdon't build a big-footprint product on Rosetta.[22m   [0m]8;;
[791] (w=71) Three reasons, independent of any removal date:                        [0m]8;;
[792] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[793] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m1. [39m[1mFragility by design.[22m Rosetta is a compatibility crutch, not a       [0m]8;;
[794] (w=71)    platform. No roadmap guarantee. Apple can trim it on any release,   [0m]8;;
[795] (w=71)    and Apple container's amd64 path is explicitly built [3mon top of[23m      [0m]8;;
[796] (w=71)    Rosetta — kill Rosetta, and the amd64+container route dies with it. [0m]8;;
[797] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m2. [39m[1mPerf tax forever.[22m Every Omarchy binary translated. That tax is      [0m]8;;
[798] (w=71)    permanent, not transitional.                                        [0m]8;;
[799] (w=71) [38;2;218;186;250m3. [39m[1mThe underlying rot is real.[22m Omarchy being x86-only is itself the    [0m]8;;
[800] (w=71)    dated part. A pure-x86 stack in a 2026+ world is a project without a[0m]8;;
[801] (w=71)    sustainable core, and you'd be strapping your build to it.          [0m]8;;
[802] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[803] (w=71) [38;2;250;204;110m[1mWhat this tilts[22m[39m                                                        [0m]8;;
[804] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[805] (w=71) The Rosetta/faithful-Omarchy route now looks like a [3mhigh-fidelity but  [0m]8;;
[806] (w=71) [3mhigh-risk[23m bet. The native [1mArch ARM[22m path isn't just a perf win — it's   [0m]8;;
[807] (w=71) the [1mdurable[22m one. You drop the dead x86 mirror dependency and rebuild   [0m]8;;
[808] (w=71) Omarchy's soul (dotfiles, config scripts, shell, theme, apps) from     [0m]8;;
[809] (w=71) arch-independent [38;2;218;186;250mconfig/[39m + vanilla Arch ARM repos.                     [0m]8;;
[810] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[811] (w=71) Cost: you don't get Omarchy's curated package set, and you do real     [0m]8;;
[812] (w=71) assembly work. But you end up with something that survives Apple's next[0m]8;;
[813] (w=71) three hardware pivots, not one.                                        [0m]8;;
[814] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[815] (w=71) If the premise is "build something I run for years," I'd bet the Arch  [0m]8;;
[816] (w=71) ARM native rebuild. If it were a month-long experiment and Rosetta     [0m]8;;
[817] (w=71) lives, the faithful route is quicker to a ship.                        [0m]8;;
[818] (w=71)                                                                        [0m]8;;
[819] (w=71) Which risk profile shapes the build: [1mdurable native rebuild (Arch ARM)[22m [0m]8;;
[820] (w=71) or [1mfaithful-but-Rosetta-fragile (amd64)[22m? I'll go build whichever you   [0m]8;;
